  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Show that the lines $y=2x+1$, $y=3x-2$ and $y=4x+6$ are not concurrent.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The first two lines intersect at $(3,7)$, but $4(3)+6=18\\ne7$, so the three lines are not concurrent.",
      "worked": "For the first two lines:\n$$\\begin{align*}2x+1&=3x-2\\\\x&=3\\end{align*}$$\nThen $y=2(3)+1=7$, so their intersection is $(3,7)$.\n\nFor the third line, $4(3)+6=18\\ne7$. Hence $(3,7)$ does not lie on the third line. The three lines have no common intersection and are not concurrent.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
